{\b Chapter 4 Research Methodology}\par
\par
{\b 4.1 Introduction}\par
This chapter explains the methodology used to examine how recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement influence student trust and perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students. The chapter presents the research design, population and sample, sampling technique, research instrument, validity and reliability procedures, data collection process, data analysis plan, and ethical considerations. The study adopts a quantitative survey-experimental design because the research problem requires a structured test of how visible credential features influence student judgments.\par
\par
{\b 4.2 Research Design}\par
This study adopts a quantitative survey experiment using a randomized `2 x 2 x 2` vignette-based design. The experimental design is appropriate because it allows the study to test how specific credential features influence student trust and perceived value while holding the base credential description constant. Rather than asking students only for general opinions about micro-credentials, the study exposes respondents to one hypothetical IT micro-credential scenario and varies three visible attributes across conditions.\par
\par
The three manipulated factors are formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement. Each factor has two levels. Formal recognition is manipulated as either present or absent. Stackability is manipulated as either present or absent. Industry endorsement is manipulated as either present or absent. This creates eight experimental conditions in total.\par
\par
The survey experiment is chosen for three main reasons. First, it aligns closely with the theoretical logic of the study, which treats visible credential features as signals. Second, it is feasible under the practical constraint that the study relies on student respondents rather than employer samples. Third, it provides stronger leverage for examining judgment under controlled conditions than a purely descriptive student opinion survey.\par
\par
{\b 4.3 Population and Sample}\par
The target population of the study consists of undergraduate and postgraduate students enrolled in IT-related programs at universities in Thailand. These programs include information technology, computer science, software engineering, information systems, computer engineering, and related digital disciplines. This population is appropriate because students in these fields are likely to face rapid changes in required knowledge and skills and may therefore have particular interest in shorter and more targeted learning credentials.\par
\par
The study aims to obtain `480` usable responses, with a preferred allocation of `60` respondents per experimental condition. A minimum acceptable sample of `320` usable responses is set, which would still allow approximately `40` respondents per condition. This target is intended to support reliable group comparisons, mediation testing, and robustness checks while remaining realistic within the expected student recruitment environment.\par
\par
{\b 4.4 Sampling Technique}\par
The study uses a multi-university purposive and convenience sampling strategy. Respondents will be recruited through university student groups, faculty networks, course channels, and online student communities where access is feasible. Purposive screening is applied to ensure that participants are enrolled in Thai universities and are studying in IT-related disciplines. Convenience elements are necessary because no complete sampling frame of all Thai IT students is readily available for random selection.\par
\par
Although this sampling strategy does not provide full national representativeness, it is appropriate for the study's design because the primary objective is to test how experimental manipulations affect student judgments. The survey experiment relies on internal comparison across randomized conditions, and random assignment helps strengthen internal validity even when the sample is not fully probabilistic.\par
\par
{\b 4.5 Research Instrument}\par
The research instrument is a structured online questionnaire designed for administration through a platform such as Qualtrics or another survey system capable of random assignment. The instrument is composed of the following parts.\par
\par
First, the questionnaire begins with introductory information and consent text explaining the purpose of the study, the voluntary nature of participation, and the approximate completion time. Second, screening questions are used to confirm that the respondent is currently enrolled in a university in Thailand, is at least 18 years old, and is studying in an IT-related field. Third, demographic and background questions collect information on level of study, university type, prior MOOC experience, prior micro-credential completion, and familiarity with the term micro-credential.\par
\par
Fourth, each respondent is randomly assigned to one of the eight vignette conditions. All respondents read the same basic scenario describing an online IT micro-credential called `Applied Data Analytics for Digital Projects`. The base description states that the credential is designed for university students and early-career learners, takes `20` hours to complete, includes online learning activities and an assessed final task, and provides a digital certificate after successful completion. The three manipulated statements then vary the presence or absence of recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement.\par
\par
Fifth, the survey includes manipulation-check items to verify whether respondents perceived the intended differences among conditions. Sixth, the questionnaire includes the student trust scale. Seventh, the questionnaire includes the perceived value scale. Eighth, the questionnaire includes an exploratory enrollment intention scale. Finally, the questionnaire includes an attention-check item and an optional short open-ended question asking respondents what most influenced their opinion of the credential.\par
\par
The trust items are adapted to the micro-credential context using the trustworthiness and credibility logic associated with Ohanian (1990). The perceived value items are adapted from higher education perceived-value logic using Alves (2010) and Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) as the main accessible sources. The vignette manipulations are self-developed for this study but are grounded in the literature on recognition, quality assurance, pathways, and stakeholder credibility.\par
\par
{\b 4.6 Validity and Reliability}\par
Several procedures will be used to support validity and reliability. First, the vignette and questionnaire wording will be reviewed for face validity and clarity before field administration. This review should focus especially on whether students clearly understand terms such as `stackability`, `formal recognition`, and `industry endorsement`. Second, a pilot test with approximately `15` to `25` students will be conducted to identify problems in wording, timing, and realism of the vignette conditions.\par
\par
Third, manipulation checks will be used to confirm that respondents noticed the intended differences in recognition, stackability, and endorsement. Fourth, internal consistency of the student trust, perceived value, and exploratory enrollment intention scales will be evaluated using Cronbach's alpha and item-total inspection. Fifth, item behavior will be reviewed to identify any weak or repetitive items before final analysis. Together, these procedures are intended to support the clarity, reliability, and construct adequacy of the instrument.\par
\par
{\b 4.7 Data Collection Procedure}\par
Data collection will be conducted through an online survey platform. After the instrument is finalized and pilot-tested, the questionnaire link will be distributed to eligible student groups and networks. Respondents will first see the consent page, then complete the screening questions, demographic items, vignette condition, and post-vignette evaluation scales.\par
\par
Recruitment will continue until the study reaches the target number of usable responses or the minimum acceptable threshold if the full target is not achievable within the available data collection period. Responses will be monitored during collection to check approximate balance across the eight experimental conditions. If a platform without automatic randomization is used, condition allocation must be carefully managed so that respondent counts remain as even as possible across groups.\par
\par
{\b 4.8 Data Analysis}\par
The data analysis will proceed in several stages. First, the dataset will be screened to remove incomplete responses, ineligible respondents, and clearly inattentive cases identified through the attention check or implausibly short completion times. Second, respondent characteristics will be summarized descriptively.\par
\par
Third, randomization checks will be conducted to compare the experimental groups on relevant background variables such as level of study, university type, prior MOOC experience, prior micro-credential experience, and familiarity with micro-credentials. Fourth, manipulation checks will be analyzed to confirm that the experimental conditions were perceived as intended.\par
\par
Fifth, reliability of the multi-item scales will be assessed. Sixth, the main hypothesis testing will be conducted. Student trust will be regressed on the three manipulated signal variables and control variables. Perceived value will then be regressed on the three manipulated signal variables, student trust, and control variables. Depending on the final distribution and coding, the analysis may use multiple regression and/or factorial ANOVA as appropriate to the study design.\par
\par
Seventh, mediation analysis will be conducted using bootstrap procedures with at least `5,000` resamples to test the indirect effects of recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement on perceived value through student trust. Finally, exploratory analysis may examine the relationship between perceived value and enrollment intention, as well as subgroup comparisons such as undergraduate versus postgraduate respondents or respondents with and without prior MOOC experience.\par
\par
{\b 4.9 Ethical Considerations}\par
This study involves voluntary participation by adult university students. Respondents will be informed that participation is optional, that responses are anonymous, and that they may stop at any time without penalty. No personally identifying information should be collected unless absolutely necessary for administrative reasons, and if such information is collected it must be separated from the analytical dataset.\par
\par
The study should ensure confidentiality of responses and restrict data access to the researcher. The vignette content is low risk because it asks respondents to evaluate a hypothetical educational credential rather than disclose sensitive personal information. Even so, ethical treatment requires clear consent language, secure data storage, and careful handling of any optional comments provided by participants.\par
\par
{\b 4.10 Chapter Summary}\par
This chapter described the methodology used in the study. The research adopts a quantitative `2 x 2 x 2` vignette-based survey experiment to test how formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement influence student trust and perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students. The chapter outlined the target population, sampling strategy, research instrument, validity and reliability procedures, data collection process, analytical strategy, and ethical considerations. The next chapter presents the results of the analysis.\par
